// ============================================================================
// CALL-EDGE-GEN · CLIENT CALL EDGES  (provenance-split resolved edges)
//
// Mirrors java/engine/call-edge-generation/calls.dl: consume the disambiguated callee
// set and split it by whether the callee is CLIENT or LIBRARY code, so a row is
// self-describing.
//
// WHAT IS NOT PORTED, and why. Java's lib-boundary gate (lib_shadowed_for /
// lib_target_kept) exists because method_name_in_type hands callee-resolution EVERY
// declaration of a name reachable from the receiver's type, and Java binds exactly the
// NEAREST one. Python's equivalent narrowing happens EARLIER and differently: the MRO's
// prec-minimality (resolution/attribute-lookup.dl) already selects the winner, and it
// does so on an ORDER rather than on a subtype test — which is the whole reason C3 is
// needed. So there is nothing left to suppress at boundary-emission time, and a copy of
// Java's gate here would be dead code pretending to be a safeguard.
// ============================================================================

// ── client_calls_client(CallerProv, SiteHash, CalleeProv, CalleeMethodHash) ──
client_calls_client("client", site, "client", callee) :-
    expr_resolves_to_method(site, callee),
    method_owner("client", _, callee).
// Module-level and nested functions have no owning class, so method_owner does not
// cover them — and they are 16% of functions plus every closure. Java has no analogue
// of this clause because Java has no code outside a class.
client_calls_client("client", site, "client", callee) :-
    expr_resolves_to_method(site, callee),
    method_module_level("client", _, callee).

// ── client_calls_lib(CallerProv, SiteHash, CalleeProv, LibMethodHash) ────────
// Reached by NAME, never by hash (resolution/lib-linking.dl). Not expanded: the chain
// ENDS at the library in a client-only build.
client_calls_lib("client", site, "lib", callee) :- call_hits_lib_method(site, callee).

// ── client_calls_builtin(CallerProv, SiteHash, TargetLabel) ──────────────────
// A CPython C-level callable: `super()`, `object.__init__` for a class with no
// constructor anywhere in its MRO. The callee is known exactly and has no Python source
// in ANY IR, so the target is a LABEL, not a hash — deliberately not hash-shaped, so it
// can never be mistaken for a py_method FK.
client_calls_builtin("client", site, label) :- call_hits_builtin(site, label).
